  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOur region remains in a warm and very humid air mass. This\nmorning's 12z KALY sounding showed a PWAT of 1.97\" and dewpoints\nin valley areas are already in the low 70s.   A slow moving \nboundary is located well west of the area towards the Great \nLakes. Meanwhile, a strong Bermuda high is located off the \neastern seaboard. As a result, there is a warm and muggy \nsoutherly flow in place over the region.\n\nDespite limited large-scale forcing, scattered showers have \nalready been developing across the region. Most of this activity\nhas been fairly shallow and there hasn't been much lightning \nactivity in our area just yet. SPC Mesoanalysis shows rather\npoor mid-level lapse rates thanks to the warm and humid air mass\nin place, with values around 5.5 deg C/km. Still, the\ncombination of warm temperatures and humid conditions is\nallowing for MLCAPE values to exceed 1000 J/kg for much of the\narea. There is some shear in place, with 0-6 km bulk shear\naround 30 kts, although most of this is thanks to stronger winds\nwithin the 3-6 km layer.\n\nWhile showers and some thunderstorms will likely continue to\ndevelop within the daytime heating, they look to be fairly\nshort-lived, as the limited forcing and weak lapse rates will\nprevent storms from getting too tall. While the shear would\nsuggest some storms could potentially organize, it will probably\nbe fairly isolated, as most storms won't get tall enough. Can't\nrule out some isolated strong wind gusts due to collapsing\nstorms, but the threat for wind damage looks fairly isolated at\nthis time. With the high PWATs, heavy downpours can be expected\nwith any shower or thunderstorm. Soils remains fairly wet across\nthe region, so the threat for isolated flash flooding remains in\nplace. Urban and poor drainage areas would be more susceptible\nto flooding today with the showers and thunderstorm activity,\nbut any area that seeing repeated storms could be at risk. \n\nThrough this evening, scattered to numerous storms will continue\nbut will start to dissipate after the loss of daytime heating\nafter sunset.  \n\nAdditional showers and thunderstorms will develop again on\nFriday. The overall pattern will stay fairly similar with our\nregion within a warm and humid air mass ahead of a slow moving\nboundary with weak overall forcing and poor lapse rates aloft.\nCan't rule out an isolated strong wind gusts, especially for\nsouthern areas on Friday, as 0-6 km bulk shear will be strongest\nfor southern areas. Some of the AI/ML guidance suggests southern\nparts of our area southward into the mid Atlantic may be at risk\nfor some wind damage on Friday. It will remain very humid with \nhigh moisture levels, so isolated flash flooding will also continue\nto be a threat if areas see repeated storms. \n\nSome convection is also possible again on Saturday, mainly\nduring the afternoon and evening hours. Flooding downpours will\ncontinue to be a threat thanks to continued high dewpoints/PWATs,\nalthough there may be less of a concern for severe winds on \nSaturday. With the upper level disturbance a little closer, \nthere may be more coverage of showers and thunderstorms on \nSaturday, especially for northern areas, although this is still \nsomewhat uncertain at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith dewpoints in the 70s, heat index values have reached Heat\nAdvisory criteria in the mid Hudson Valley already today. Heat\nAdvisory remains in effect for Eastern Ulster, Western Dutchess\nand Southern Litchfield County for the rest of today and\ntomorrow through 8 PM. Heat Index values may reach the mid to\nupper 90s in these areas through this evening and again on\nFriday thanks to very dewpoints (lower to middle 70s) and highs\nin the mid 80s to low 90s. This will lead to an increased risk\nof heat-related illnesses. Even outside of the Advisory area,\nhigh dewpoints and warm temps could be hazardous, so everyone is\nencouraged to practice heat safety and take breaks in the\nshade and drink plenty of water. Similar temps/dewpoints will be\nin place again Friday. Dewpoints may be slightly lower by\nSaturday, which may help avoid needing additional heat\nheadlines, although there will be a more noticeable drop in\ndewpoints by Sunday.",
